    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Feminism holds that modern societies are patriarchal, and since the late 18th century its movements have campaigned for women’s right to vote, hold office, work, earn equal pay, own property and receive education.</li><li>Its Western history is told in waves: suffrage movements in the 19th and early 20th centuries, the women’s liberation movement from the 1960s, a third wave from about 1992 and a fourth, of social media, from about 2012.</li><li>Liberal, socialist and radical feminism, the “Big Three”, have been joined by Black, postcolonial, intersectional and other feminisms, born largely of the criticism that the movement spoke for white, middle-class women.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The word began as a term of disapproval: “féminisme” first appeared in an 1871 French medical thesis on men with tuberculosis who had developed feminine traits, and in 1872 Alexandre Dumas fils coined “féministe” for men who supported women’s rights.</p></aside>
    </section>

